WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.160
Trace the fate of a single photon in three panels.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:07.800
First, absorption.

3
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.220
Only a photon with the right energy can promote an electron in the pigment from the

4
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.000
ground state to an excited state.

5
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.320
Second, what happens if that energy is not captured quickly enough?

6
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.640
The excited electron simply relaxes, releasing the energy as heat or fluorescence,

7
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.420
and the opportunity is lost.

8
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.880
Third, the productive route at the reaction center.

9
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.380
The excited pigment hands its electron to a primary acceptor, creating charge

10
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.460
separation. That moment, where an electron physically moves rather than the energy

11
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.040
merely transferring, is where photosynthesis truly begins.

12
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.690
Light harvesting and chemistry are two distinct jobs, and this funnel shows the

13
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.170
handoff. On the left, in the antenna system, two to three hundred pigments pass

14
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.650
excitation energy from donor to acceptor by resonance transfer.

15
00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.870
The energy hops, but the electrons themselves stay put, and efficiency falls off

16
00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:02.870
steeply with distance.

17
00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:07.770
All of that captured energy is funneled into a single reaction center on the right.

18
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.050
There P680 or P700 finally does something different.

19
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.650
It gives up an actual electron to an acceptor.

20
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.430
Energy transfer concentrates the harvest.

21
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.470
Electron transfer in the reaction center commits it to chemistry.

22
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:27.233
Here is the whole light reaction circuit drawn across the thylakoid membrane.

23
00:01:28.013 --> 00:01:29.013
Start at the left.

24
00:01:29.273 --> 00:01:33.913
Water is split at photosystem II, releasing oxygen and protons and feeding electrons

25
00:01:33.913 --> 00:01:40.413
into the chain. Light excites P680, then P700 at photosystem I, lifting electrons

26
00:01:40.413 --> 00:01:46.213
twice. As electrons flow, protons accumulate inside the thylakoid lumen, building a

27
00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:47.253
concentration gradient.

28
00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:51.833
NADP plus is reduced to NADPH on the stromal side.

29
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:57.133
Finally, that proton gradient drives ATP synthase, which makes ATP.

30
00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:02.993
In one picture, light in at two photosystems, water split, protons pumped, and the

31
00:02:02.993 --> 00:02:07.293
two energy currencies, and ATP, produced together.

32
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:11.923
The two photosystems work as partners in a choreographed two-step.

33
00:02:12.723 --> 00:02:13.723
Photosystem II leads.

34
00:02:14.423 --> 00:02:19.083
It splits water molecules and releases the oxygen we breathe, and its electrons drop

35
00:02:19.083 --> 00:02:22.743
in energy to build a proton gradient that powers ATP synthesis.

36
00:02:23.723 --> 00:02:24.923
Photosystem I follows.

37
00:02:25.703 --> 00:02:31.323
A second hit of light re-energizes the electrons so they can produce NADPH, the

38
00:02:31.323 --> 00:02:34.323
reducing power that ultimately enables sugar production.

39
00:02:35.063 --> 00:02:40.223
Read the energy diagram as two uphill jumps powered by light, with a downhill slide

40
00:02:40.223 --> 00:02:42.003
between them that pumps protons.

41
00:02:42.603 --> 00:02:47.243
Two light-driven boosts, one to make ATP, one to make NADPH.

42
00:02:48.417 --> 00:02:52.417
Let us tally exactly what Photosystem II does in one balanced statement.

43
00:02:53.097 --> 00:02:56.717
Inputs on the left: four photons of light and two molecules of water.

44
00:02:57.337 --> 00:03:01.437
The machine in the middle, with its P680 reaction center, splits that water.

45
00:03:02.157 --> 00:03:03.857
The outputs on the right are remarkable.

46
00:03:04.677 --> 00:03:09.957
Electrons leave on plastoquinol, the QH2 carrier, headed for cytochrome b6f.

47
00:03:09.957 --> 00:03:13.577
Four protons are deposited in the lumen, feeding the gradient.

48
00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:19.457
And oxygen gas is released, arguably the most important waste product in history.

49
00:03:20.257 --> 00:03:25.637
One machine, four photons, two waters, and the breathable atmosphere is a byproduct

50
00:03:25.637 --> 00:03:27.857
of charging the electron transport chain.
